      <h2>Georeferencing Our Collections</h2>

      <h3>What is georeferencing?</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Georeferencing</strong> is the process of aligning a scanned historical map with real-world geographic
        coordinates. Once a map is georeferenced, it can be treated as geospatial data, opening up possibilities like:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Layering a historic map on top of a modern web map</li>
        <li>Overlaying one historic map on another to compare how a place has changed over time</li>
        <li>Transcribing geospatial data from historic maps, such as labels, features, or outlines</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        As a user, you'll most often encounter georeferenced maps in interactive web viewers, where a historic map is
        displayed on top of a modern base map so you can see the two side by side.
      </p>

      <h3>Using Georeferenced Maps</h3>
      <p>Many of Texas A&M's historical maps have been georeferenced using <a href="https://allmaps.org/">Allmaps</a>.
        When a map has been georeferenced, a <strong>Georeferenced Map</strong> tab will appear beneath the image.
        Clicking this tab displays the map superimposed on a modern base map in the Allmaps Viewer, where you can
        adjust the transparency to compare the historic and modern geography.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="/themes/custom/tamu_theme/images/georeferenced.png" title="Example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba" alt="An example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba" style="max-width:720px;height:auto;background-color:#800000;padding:.25em">
        <figcaption>An example of a Georeferenced Map of Cuba</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3>Contributing to Our Georeferencing in Allmaps</h3>
      <p>Georeferencing works by matching points on a map image to points on a modern map; these are called
      <em>ground control points</em>, or simply <em>control points</em>. We've already created control points for
      hundreds of our maps, but you can help by georeferencing new maps or improving the georeferencing on maps that
      already have it.</p>
      <p>Texas A&M uses the <a href="https://allmaps.org">Allmaps platform</a> for georeferencing. Allmaps makes the
      process easier for contributors, more interoperable across institutional collections, and simpler to maintain.
      Unlike traditional georeferencing software, ,which can be complicated to use and often requires downloading and
      uploading very large image files, Allmaps runs almost entirely in a user's web browser. This lowers the barrier
      for anyone who wants to create or view georeferenced maps, and reduces the need for institutions to maintain
      expensive, brittle server infrastructure.</p>
      <p>To georeference a map (or improve its existing georeferencing), start by finding its IIIF manifest. You can
      find the link in the upper right corner above the viewer, or under <strong>"View IIIF Manifest"</strong> at the
        bottom of the <em>Description</em> tab. Copy the manifest URL and paste it into the
      <a href="https://editor.allmaps.org">Allmaps Editor</a></p>
      <p>If the item contains multiple images, draw a mask around each image that includes a map. Once you've drawn your
      masks and selected a map, georeference it by connecting three or more points on the image to their corresponding
      real-world locations. Once you're done, the georeferenced map will automatically appear in our digital collections
      portal within 24 hours.</p>
      <figure>
        <img src="/themes/custom/tamu_theme/images/allmaps-edit.png" title="Example of a Georeferenced Map of Texas" alt="An example of a Georeferenced Map of Texas" style="max-width:720px;height:auto;background-color:#800000;padding:.25em">
        <figcaption><a href="https://editor.allmaps.org/georeference?url=https%3A%2F%2Fdigitalcollections.library.tamu.edu%2Fiiif%2F2%2F1f7%252Fimage-00004381467-18304958-0487-4ffe-a760-508aa8aa06fc.jp2%2Finfo.json&image=https%3A%2F%2Fdigitalcollections.library.tamu.edu%2Fiiif%2F2%2F1f7%252Fimage-00004381467-18304958-0487-4ffe-a760-508aa8aa06fc.jp2">Using Allmaps Editor to Georeference a Historic Map of Texas</a></figcaption>
      </figure>
